note('Author page 11, archive number 29. The page adds two poles and meridian arcs, presents IV_J with six relations, and writes a G_J-equivariant endomorphism in terms of tilde-alpha and a parameter read here as zeta. Equation (37) asserts tilde-alpha = zeta rho^{-1}(zeta^{-1}); the margin says there is no condition on zeta here. This assertion is transcribed, not independently proved. The indexing phrase actually reads i in omega although Q_i previously uses J; the opening P label looks like P_i^omega whereas later poles carry infinity. These discrepancies are retained. The sphere’s two right-hand equatorial arrowheads both point towards Q_0; this is also retained, without repairing orientation by analogy. The angular parameter is provisionally fixed as zeta throughout the page; cross-page notation comparison remains necessary. No mathematical claim was checked beyond identifying the indexing inconsistency by the stated definitions.')
check('030-indexing-Q','used and rechecked in this run','Compared the three named Q vertices and preceding J notation with the literal i in omega; noted the mismatch without altering omega.')
hand('Angular zeta and compressed subscripts','The parameter’s top is a small angular zig and its lower part one descending curve; C calls it xi, H zeta. Adopt zeta consistently on this page, medium confidence, to be compared with later definitions. This does not yet establish a universal habit for every Greek squiggle. Pole subscripts vary in clarity; the two in (34) remain unresolved and must not be deleted as H does.')
